<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liu Kun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">271–318</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Liu Kun was a Western Jin general, poet and governor of Bingzhou in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> who held out against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-zhao">Han Zhao</a> after the collapse of Jin authority in the north. He relied on alliances with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba">Tuoba</a> chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba-yilu">Tuoba Yilu</a>, whom Jin made Duke and then Prince of Dai, and with the Duan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a>. In 318 the Duan chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duan-pidi">Duan Pidi</a> had him killed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liu-kun.jpg" alt="Liu Kun" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 中国新闻网 · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liu_Kun_2019.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
